---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('high-school-computing-programs-alberta');

const faqJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  'mainEntity': [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'What computing competitions can Alberta high school students enter?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'The Canadian Computing Competition is written at your school on February 17, 2027 with registration closing February 11. Others include CyberTitan, NASA\'s International Space Apps Challenge, the Swift Student Challenge, CyberSci capture-the-flag events, and Hack the North.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Which computing programs does a teacher have to register you for?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'About half the list. The Canadian Computing Competition, CyberTitan, Amii\'s K-12 AI literacy kits and the Cisco Networking Academy all run through a teacher or a school board rather than through a student form. If your school does not do any of them, that is worth knowing in September rather than in April, because all four are things one teacher can start.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'When does CyberTitan registration close?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'Teachers register the team between April and October for a competition that runs October to May. Ask in the spring before the year you want to compete.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'What computing competitions can I enter without my school?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'Four, and three of them are free: NASA\'s International Space Apps Challenge, the Swift Student Challenge for under-18s, CyberSci capture-the-flag events, and Hack the North, which covers travel, food and lodging for accepted students.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Are there computing programs specifically for girls in Alberta?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'Yes, and both are underused here. Technovation Girls runs January to May for ages 13 to 18, building and pitching a mobile app or AI solution to industry judges. Hackergal\'s National Ambassador Program covers Grades 9 to 12, and taking part qualifies you for Hackergal\'s scholarship for graduating students.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Is a free coding contest better than a paid summer program?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'For an application, generally yes. A tuition-based summer program proves you could afford it. A contest result, a certification or a shipped app proves you did something under a constraint somebody else set, which is what a selection committee is trying to establish.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJson)} slot="ld" />

  <p>
    In computing, several of the strongest things a high school student can put on an application are free contests. Four on this page are entered by a teacher, not by you, so for those the first step is asking a teacher.
  </p>

  <h2>The ones a teacher signs you up for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/programs/canadian-computing-competition-ccc/">The Canadian Computing Competition</a></strong> is run by Waterloo's CEMC. It is written at your school on February 17, 2027, and registration closes February 11. Top scorers are invited to the Canadian Computing Olympiad. If your school has never run it, a teacher can register.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/programs/cybertitan-national-cybersecurity-competition/">CyberTitan</a></strong> is a national cyber defence competition for teams of up to fifteen, running October to May. Teachers register the team between April and October, so ask in the spring before the year you want to compete.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/programs/amii-k-12-ai-literacy/">Amii's K–12 AI literacy kits</a></strong> are free, curriculum-aligned, and delivered to Alberta classrooms by the province's own machine intelligence institute. You cannot apply. A science or CTS teacher requests one, so asking is the entire mechanism.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/programs/cisco-networking-academy-high-school-stream/">The Cisco Networking Academy</a></strong> runs through participating Alberta school boards as a semester or year-long course, and it ends in industry-recognised certifications in networking and IT fundamentals.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Ask in September, not April. All four are things one teacher can start.
  </p>

  <h2>The ones you enter yourself</h2>
  <p>
    You register for these yourself, and three of the four are free.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/programs/nasa-international-space-apps-challenge/">NASA's International Space Apps Challenge</a></strong> is the largest hackathon in the world and it is free. The 2026 edition runs November 14–15, registration opens August 26, and Alberta hubs ran in both Calgary and Edmonton last year. Challenges are written by NASA staff and span software, data science, hardware and design, so a team does not have to be all programmers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/programs/apple-swift-student-challenge/">The Swift Student Challenge</a></strong> asks for one thing: an original Swift playground you built. It is open to under-18s, it is judged on the work rather than on your record, and winners get WWDC access and developer tools. It is the most portfolio-like item on this page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/programs/cybersci-cyber-security-challenge-canada-high-school/">CyberSci</a></strong> runs capture-the-flag events (network vulnerabilities, cryptography, server security) at regional and national levels, open to teams or individuals. If CyberTitan's teacher window has already closed, this is the way into the same skill set this year.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/programs/hack-the-north-high-school-track/">Hack the North</a></strong> is Canada's largest hackathon, 36 hours at Waterloo with more than a thousand students, and travel, food and lodging are covered for those accepted. The application is competitive and it reopens in the summer.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The year-long ones you can do from anywhere</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/programs/qubit-by-qubit-high-school-quantum-computing/">Qubit by Qubit</a> teaches quantum computing to high school students over a full academic year, taught by PhD researchers working with Google Quantum AI, with need-based aid available and no prior quantum or coding experience assumed. The signature introductory course starts in October. <a href="/programs/ibm-skillsbuild-for-high-school/">IBM SkillsBuild</a> is self-paced and free, and issues badges in AI, web development and cloud computing that you can point at.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your maths and physics are strong, <a href="/programs/quantum-school-for-young-students-qsys/">QSYS</a> at Waterloo's Institute for Quantum Computing takes Grade 11 and 12 students for a week in August, with applications typically closing in mid-May. It is small and in person.
  </p>

  <h2>If you are a girl</h2>
  <p>
    Two programs are for girls only. <a href="/programs/technovation-girls-alberta/">Technovation Girls</a> runs January to May for ages 13 to 18: your team finds a community problem, does the user research, builds a mobile app or an AI solution, and pitches it to industry judges. It works remotely or through a local Alberta hub.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/programs/hackergal-national-ambassador-program/">Hackergal's National Ambassador Program</a> continues where Hackergal's hackathon stops: the hackathon ends at Grade 9, and the ambassador program takes Grades 9 to 12. Ambassadors run their own clubs and workshops and move through four yearly stages. Taking part also qualifies you for Hackergal's scholarship for graduating students, which reopens in November 2026. The intake is closed at the moment with no reopening date posted, so this one is a calendar reminder rather than an application.
  </p>

  <h2>What these do for an application</h2>
  <p>
    A contest result, a certification or an app you shipped is work measured against someone else's standard, which a paid course is not. That is the evidence to bring to the awards on our <a href="/scholarships/stem/">STEM scholarships</a> list.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you want a lab rather than a contest, the paid research placements are covered in <a href="/guides/high-school-research-programs-alberta/">research programs for Alberta students</a>. Otherwise the full list is on the <a href="/programs/computing/">computing and CS programs page</a>. <a href="/match/">Build my combo</a> cuts it to the ones your grade can enter.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
